  "modern": "SILICERNIUM, among the Romans was a fault of a private nature, provided for the dead some time after the funeral. It consisted of beans, lettuce, bread, eggs, &c. Their eyes laid upon the tomb, and they possibly believed that the dead would come out for the repast. What was left was generally burnt on the floor. The word _Alternate_ as derived from _Place and_ crear, i. e. of figure upon a fleece. Eating what had thus been provided for the dead, was esteemed a mark of the most miserable poverty. A similar entertainment was made by the Greeks at the tombs of the deceased; but it was usual among them to treat the ghosts with the fragments from the feast of the living. See FUNERAL AND INTERMENT."
